<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Māhele</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Great Mahele</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Māhele was the land division of 1848 in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, carried out under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a>. It divided the land, previously held under the chiefs, among the crown, the government and the chiefs, and later laws allowed commoners and, from 1850, foreigners to own land outright. Within decades much land had passed to foreign, mainly American, owners and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-mahele.jpg" alt="Great Māhele" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kalama, Simona P.; Lahainaluna Seminary · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Na_mokupuni_o_Hawaii_nei_LOC_98680907.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Before the Māhele, Hawaiian chiefs administered the land, but ordinary people were free to move on and off it, paid tribute to the ruling chiefs and used its natural resources. After the Laplace Affair of 1839, advisers of King Kauikeaouli Kamehameha III sought to protect the rights of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/native-hawaiians">Native Hawaiians</a>. The Hawaiian Bill of Rights of 1839, drafted by Boaz Mahune, guaranteed that people would not be forced off their land without cause, though not the right to acquire it, and the 1840 Constitution made the kingdom a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> and declared that the land belonged to its people and was managed by the king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1845 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a> set up the Land Commission to judge claims to land made by natives and foreigners alike; it reviewed claims but distributed no land. After it had examined and honoured many claims, the king met his chiefs and the commission, and the Māhele came into effect on 7 March 1848. It replaced a system in which the king entrusted the care of the land to high chiefs and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konohiki">konohiki</a>, the managers of each <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahupua-a">ahupuaʻa</a>, who drew taxes and tribute from those who worked it.</p><p>Two laws of 1850 followed. On 10 July the Alien Land Ownership Act, written by Chief Justice William Little Lee, allowed foreigners to hold title to land, and on 6 August the Kuleana Act allowed commoners to claim the land they lived on and cultivated, while ending their rights of cultivation and pasturage on the larger common lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A claim required money for a survey and two witnesses, and many commoners were never told of their right or were unfamiliar with private ownership. About 18,000 plots, some 28,658 acres or less than 1% of the land, were claimed, at a time when the native population was about 82,000. Members of the higher classes and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alii">aliʻi</a> obtained most of the land, many claimants later lost theirs to disease and property taxes, and the government of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> sold most of the land to settlers from the continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> or to the Big Five corporations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Māhele” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Great Māhele</b> of 1848 divided the land among the king, the government and the chiefs, and laws of 1850 let commoners claim the plots they farmed and foreigners buy land.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In Hawaii the chiefs became the nobility of a kingdom whose <b>Great Māhele</b> of 1848 divided their lands and opened the way to private ownership, and conquest ended most chiefdoms of the Americas centuries earlier.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
